Machine profiles: the plural, and a binding that discovers rather than declares

> Work on the profiles first.

`machines/` — one directory per host this repository can plausibly run on, so
that an agent arriving on one does not have to work the environment out.

## The plural is the actual work

`station-node` has one of these and its own notes say why that shape is
provisional: *"it is not labelled a profile, because there is exactly one and a
category with one member looks like a thing rather than a kind."*

FCPM is where the kind gets a second member — two bays, both expected to run
this, possibly **at once**. That changes the question a checkout has to answer:

| | asks | because |
|---|---|---|
| `station-node` | has a profile been put on? | there is only one it could be |
| here | has one been put on, **and which?** | there are two, simultaneously |

## Which profile a checkout wears is discovered, never declared

This falls out of your own rule rather than being invented for it:

> Anything declared is copied by the act that makes it wrong.

A committed file saying *this checkout is bay one* travels to bay two the moment
somebody clones it, and is then a confident lie. So no such file exists.

- Each profile carries **`names`** — what that host should answer to. Intent.
- The machine answers `scutil --get LocalHostName`. Fact.
- **The profile worn is the one whose intent matches the fact.**
  `machines/binding` reports it.

Config describes intent; the system discovers fact. Same house rule as `names`
and `bin/binding` upstream.

### Proven, all three paths

Run here, on a machine that is neither bay:

```
this machine answers to: Autumns-iMac

  editing-bay-1        unfilled — nobody has asked that machine its name
  editing-bay-2        unfilled — nobody has asked that machine its name

wearing: nothing.
A clone with no profile is not a broken station. It is data.
```

Exit **0**, because unclaimed is the clean state and an exit code is a claim
too. Temporarily filling one `names` produced `wearing: editing-bay-1`, exit 0.
Filling both produced *"More than one profile claims this machine … that is a
contradiction in `machines/`, not something to guess past"*, exit **1** — a
mistake in the files is not something to resolve at run time by picking one.

System python, no dependencies. These are machines whose job is editing video.

## Both profiles claim nothing, and that is a statement

Under the borrowed rule — *a folder in a profile is a claim to have the thing it
names* — an empty profile directory says there is nothing here yet established
as ours to configure. Neither bay has any folders. **Do not add one to make a
profile look complete;** add it when the thing is real.

`names` is unfilled in both, so `binding` matches nothing today. That is
deliberate: **nobody has stood in front of either machine and asked it what it
answers to**, and guessing it from the sticker on the front is exactly the kind
of declared-and-wrong this design is avoiding.

## What is in them is honest about what is missing

The one real fact is yours: bay 2 *"is already empowered to do a couple of
things, like schedule our Cablecast stuff."* That is recorded, along with the
observation that **what exactly, and how it is invoked, is written down nowhere**
— which is the most valuable unknown on either machine, and the kind of thing
this directory exists to stop being true.

The bay-general facts — browser profiles instead of accounts, the ~32 GB drive
partitioned eight ways, a checkout being a backup because the repo is public —
are recorded once rather than in both, because two copies of a fact drift and
the drift is invisible.

Everything else is a list of questions for whoever is first in front of each
machine. All observations, no decisions.

## Not decided

- **The directory names are placeholders** — what you call them out loud.
  Renaming is free while they are empty and expensive after.
- **Neither bay is an FCPM station node**, and one that becomes one gains
  folders rather than a different kind of profile.
- **Claiming is not a signed act here.** The prototype wants putting a profile on
  to be a commit with the right signers, and that provisioning does not exist on
  either node. Matching on `names` is weaker, is what can be built today, and
  the README says plainly it must not be allowed to become a substitute.
